From 986af73cb1c3d6135230643974b00697270fdb27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 1 Jul 2026 12:34:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05304/11362] [outer-loop] retract construction-artifact FoVer
 headroom claim; run the corrected well-posed follow-up

Operator: 'let's escalate the FoVer nudge by tackling here in the outer loop.'
Before building anything, investigated why the FoVer nudge (3 milestones,
never picked up) hadn't landed -- and found the underlying claim was never
real.

RETRACTION: 'FoVer oracle@K=0.769, SC=0.269, +0.500 headroom' (the entire
premise of MOAT REDIRECT 2026-06-30 and the NUDGE) is a construction
artifact of load_fover_domain_pool's mode=task_index%4 formula, not a
measurement -- mode0 (vote-correct) fraction = 7/26 = 0.26923..., mode!=3
(oracle-present) fraction = 20/26 = 0.76923..., matching the reported
numbers to 4 decimal places BY CONSTRUCTION. The real corpus
(data/fover_corpus_v4.json, 6548 rows) has no natural multi-candidate
structure: 6544/6546 question_ids have exactly ONE row. The pool-builder
manufactures fake candidate groups by grafting unrelated wrong steps onto a
real correct one, then hand-assigns the vote winner via the mode formula.
'Verifier beats self-consistency' does not apply to FoVer's real task shape
(flat per-step classification, not K-candidate selection).

Corrected both known-issues.md entries with prominent RETRACTED banners
(prose preserved per never-prune) and added a linter_flag_corrigendum to
headroom_survey_cross_domain.json's fover row (also noted: arc/arcgen rows
show the same suspicious mode%4 signature, though their circular-oracle
exclusion already made this moot for those two).

RAN the corrected, well-posed follow-up instead of just drafting it: does a
learned step-verifier beat a cheap text-statistical baseline at FoVer step
classification (the question the real corpus actually supports)? Real GPU
compute (all-MiniLM-L6-v2 embeddings, 3.9s, reproducible across 3 runs).
RESULT: verifier AUROC 0.9663 vs cheap-baseline AUROC 0.9635, delta CI95
[-0.0244, 0.0347] -- includes 0, does NOT beat the cheap baseline. Traced
root cause: incorrect steps average ~5x longer than correct steps (447.6 vs
84.8 chars) -- a corpus-construction confound a cheap baseline nearly
matches via length alone. Second consecutive honest negative on FoVer (after
the retracted headroom claim) for a different, more fundamental reason.

adversarial_verify: 0 flagged on the final artifact (fixed an initial
METHODOLOGY_MISSING warn by declaring the correct substrate --
verifier_ensemble_against_cached_candidates, matching the
exp_phase_d_musr_verifier_train.py precedent -- plus model_specs and a
reproducibility_checksum).

Net: the decisive oracle-distinct-headroom-present moat question remains
genuinely open. Neither MuSR (no headroom) nor FoVer (no value over a cheap
baseline) has produced a clean win.

